I pick **candidate 1**. This is a judgment from rough mental arithmetic, not an exact day-by-day simulation of each plan.

**How the plans differ.** Candidate 6 orders exactly the forecast: the day-t order equals the forecast demand on the day it arrives (t+5 for good 0, t+4 for good 1). The others are scaled versions of it:
- candidate 0 orders about 0.9× the forecast;
- candidate 4 about 1.05×;
- candidates 1 and 3 about 1.1×;
- candidate 7 about 1.2×;
- candidate 2 orders about 1.1× of good 0 but about 1.25× of good 1;
- candidate 5 orders every other day in double batches, so it pays the 22 fee less often but holds much more stock.

The shortages on days 1–4 come from the starting stock, which is too small, so they are the same for every plan.

**Why plans at or below the forecast lose.** Average actual demand is about 1.046× the forecast. Each missing unit of good 1 costs 9 in lost margin plus a 4 penalty, 13 in total. Each extra unit costs only 0.3 per day to store, and leftover stock carries over to later days. For good 0, a missing unit costs 6 (3 margin plus 3 penalty), so it pays to order a little above the average there too. That rules out candidates 0 and 6. Candidate 4 is close to the average and still runs short often.

**Why heavy over-ordering loses.**
- Candidate 7 builds about 7 extra units of good 0 per day. Storing them costs 0.9 per unit per day, and the excess spoils after 8 days.
- Candidate 2 builds about 3 extra units of good 1 per day, which spoil after 7 days.
- Candidate 5 holds far more stock because of its big batches. Storage and spoilage cost more than it saves on order fees.

**Candidate 1 versus candidate 3.** The two plans are the same up to day 19. On days 20–24, candidate 1 goes back to ordering the forecast amount, which uses up the buffer it has built. Candidate 3 instead raises its orders. Those orders arrive on days 25–29, and anything left at the end is worth nothing, so the extra units are wasted.

**Under random demand,** candidate 1's roughly 10% margin above the forecast covers typical swings in demand. It doesn't pile up stock that spoils, and storage has room for the day-9 spike in good 0.

I picked candidate 1 by comparing order sizes against the forecast. I did no simulation, so this is a judgement call, not a computed profit.

1. **What each plan tracks.** Candidate 6 orders exactly the forecast for the arrival day: mu[t+5] for item 0 and mu[t+4] for item 1. The other plans are scaled versions of it:
   - candidate 0 is about 0.9×;
   - candidate 4 is about 1.05× for item 0 and about 1.22× for item 1;
   - candidate 1 is about 1.1× for item 0 and about 1.3× for item 1;
   - candidate 7 is about 1.2× and about 1.4×;
   - candidate 2 is about 1.1× and about 1.5×;
   - candidate 5 orders roughly two days' worth every other day, paying the 22 fee half as often but holding more stock.
2. **Item 0.** A missed sale costs 6 (3 lost margin plus a 3 penalty). An extra unit is mostly kept for later and costs 0.9 per day, with a risk of spoiling after 8 days. Demand averages about 1.046× the forecast, so a modest buffer of about 1.1× makes sense. Ordering much more would build up stock over time.
3. **Item 1.** A missed sale costs 13 (9 margin plus a 4 penalty). Holding costs only 0.3 per day, so a larger buffer of about 1.3× pays off. Going to 1.4–1.5× would push more stock past its 7-day shelf life and waste it.
4. **End of the month.** Leftover stock is worth nothing after day 29. From day 20, candidate 1 cuts item 0 back to roughly the forecast (40 vs 40). Candidate 3 is identical until then but keeps ordering more at the end, so it would end with more unsold stock.

The 232-unit warehouse limit doesn't bind for any of these plans, even with the 136-unit demand on day 9.
